@page "/guides/theming"
@using Trailblazor.Docs.Examples

<PageTitle>Theming · Trailblazor</PageTitle>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Headline" As="TextElement.H1">Theming</TbText>
<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Subtitle" class="docs-lead">
    Every component draws its colors, spacing, shapes and type from design tokens: CSS custom properties named
    <code>--tb-*</code>. Attributes switch between the ready-made looks, and your own CSS can change any token.
</TbText>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Light and dark</TbText>
<TbText>
    Put <code>TbThemeHead</code> in <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, next to <code>TbStyles</code>. It starts the page in the
    mode, palette and density you set in <code>TrailblazorOptions.Theme</code>, or in the ones the user chose last
    time, and does so before the page paints, so a dark page never flashes light. <code>System</code>, the default,
    follows the device's setting. Without <code>TbThemeHead</code>, Trailblazor keeps the page's own color scheme, so
    adding the library never darkens an app by itself.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="App.razor and Program.cs" Code="@Theme" />
<TbText>
    A standalone WebAssembly app has no server to render <code>&lt;head&gt;</code>, so copy the script that
    <code>TbThemeHead</code> would render into <code>index.html</code> yourself, as this site does. There the script's
    attributes, not <code>TrailblazorOptions.Theme</code>, choose how the first page looks, so keep the two the same.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="index.html" Code="@StandaloneTheme" />
<TbText>
    The <a href="components/theme-picker">theme picker</a> lets people choose, as the one at the top of this site
    does, and <code>IThemeService</code> changes the mode, palette or density from your own code. Both remember the
    choice in the browser unless <code>Remember</code> is off.
</TbText>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Palettes</TbText>
<TbText>
    A palette sets the colors for both light and dark. Graphite, a near-monochrome look with ink-colored primary
    actions, is the default. The others are
    @string.Join(", ", PaletteCatalog.All.Skip(1).Select(palette => palette.Name)), and the
    <a href="guides/palettes">palette gallery</a> shows each one on real components. Choose the page's palette with the
    <code>Palette</code> value in <code>TrailblazorOptions.Theme</code>, or with
    <code>IThemeService.SetPaletteAsync</code>. For one part of a page, set <code>data-tb-palette</code> or
    <code>data-tb-theme</code> on any element; it applies to everything inside it. An element with
    <code>data-tb-theme</code> also takes that theme's background and text color, and <code>TbThemeHead</code> marks
    the page itself, so the page follows the theme without CSS of its own. To give the page another background, set
    it on <code>html</code> or change <code>--tb-color-background</code>; a background on <code>body</code> alone
    leaves the theme's color around it.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="A palette for part of a page" Code="@Palette" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Typography</TbText>
<TbText>
    Every component sets its text in <code>--tb-font-family</code>, the system's interface font unless you change it.
    The page that <code>TbThemeHead</code> marks takes the same font and <code>--tb-line-height-normal</code>, and its
    <code>body</code> takes <code>--tb-font-size-md</code>, so text outside the components matches them without CSS
    of your own.
</TbText>
<TbText>
    To use your own font, set <code>--tb-font-family</code> rather than a font on <code>body</code>: the token changes
    the page and every component at once, while a font on <code>body</code> alone changes only your own text and
    leaves the components in the system font.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Your own font, everywhere" Code="@Font" />
<TbText>
    To keep the page's type your own instead, set it on <code>html</code> or <code>body</code> in your stylesheet. The
    themed page's type sits in a cascade layer and uses zero-specificity selectors, so any rule of yours outside a
    cascade layer wins. Text fields, tabs, menu items and the other controls that take the text's font set their own
    line height, so a line height of yours doesn't change how tall they are.
</TbText>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Density</TbText>
<TbText>
    Compact density makes controls, items and rows shorter, for screens full of data. Comfortable, the default, leaves
    room for touch. Every pointer target stays at least 24 by 24 px in both. Choose the page's density with the
    <code>Density</code> value in <code>TrailblazorOptions.Theme</code>, or change it while the app runs with
    <code>IThemeService.SetDensityAsync</code>, which remembers the choice as it does the mode and palette. The density
    menu at the top of this site switches every page, examples included.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Program.cs" Code="@DensityOption" />
<TbText>
    For one part of a page, set <code>data-tb-density</code> on any element or component; it applies to everything
    inside it. Set <code>comfortable</code> inside a compact page to switch that part back. A virtualized tree view
    keeps the row height its <code>ItemSize</code> gives, so set that to 28 in compact density.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="A compact region" Code="@DensityRegion" />
<TbText>
    Density sets seven tokens, and the components size themselves from them, so changing one changes every component
    that uses it. The small and large heights are worked out from <code>--tb-control-height</code> only where a density
    is set: on the page or on a <code>data-tb-density</code> region. To change the control height on an element of
    your own, set <code>--tb-control-height-sm</code> and <code>--tb-control-height-lg</code> there too.
</TbText>
<TbTable aria-label="Density tokens">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th scope="col">Token</th>
            <th scope="col">Comfortable</th>
            <th scope="col">Compact</th>
            <th scope="col">Sizes</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        @foreach (var token in DensityTokens)
        {
            <tr>
                <th scope="row"><code>@token.Name</code></th>
                <td>@token.Comfortable</td>
                <td>@token.Compact</td>
                <td>@token.Sizes</td>
            </tr>
        }
    </tbody>
</TbTable>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Changing tokens</TbText>
<TbText>
    Trailblazor's CSS sits in cascade layers named <code>tb.*</code>, and your own CSS outside any layer always wins
    over it, whatever the selectors. So set any token in your stylesheet to change it everywhere.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Token overrides" Code="@Overrides" />
<TbText>
    The primary's hover, pressed, subtle and text colors and the focus ring are worked out from
    <code>--tb-color-primary</code> wherever it is set: on the page, in a palette, or in an element's
    <code>style</code>. So one color re-themes a part of a page, states included. If you set the primary from a class
    in your stylesheet instead, also put an empty <code>data-tb-palette</code> attribute on that element so its states
    follow. You can still set any of the derived tokens yourself, on the same element as the primary.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="A primary for part of a page" Code="@RegionPrimary" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Restyling one component</TbText>
<TbText>
    To change one component rather than the whole theme, reach for these in order. First, its own tokens, such as
    <code>--tb-button-radius</code>, listed under Design tokens on its page: set one on the page to change the component
    everywhere, or on an element to change it only inside that element. Next, its <code>Classes</code> parameter, a
    <code>ButtonClasses</code> or <code>FieldClasses</code> record for example, which adds your class names to its
    parts. Last, its <code>data-part</code> names, also on its page, and the ARIA attributes that mark its states, such
    as <code>aria-selected</code>, <code>aria-invalid</code> and <code>aria-disabled</code>, which let your stylesheet
    reach one part in one state.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="app.css" Code="@Restyle" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Try a brand color</TbText>
<TbText>
    Pick a color to see it as the primary, with the hover, pressed, selected and focus colors that follow from it, then
    copy the CSS. If the color is too light for readable text on it in light mode, or too dark in dark mode, it is
    darkened or lightened just that far; otherwise it is used as picked.
</TbText>
<TbColorPicker Label="Brand color" @bind-Value="_brand" />
<div class="docs-brand" style="@($"--tb-color-primary: {BrandPrimary}")">
    <TbStack Row Gap="2" Wrap Align="Align.Center">
        <TbButton>Save</TbButton>
        <TbButton Variant="Variant.Soft">Preview</TbButton>
        <TbCheckbox TValue="bool" Label="Share with team" Value="true" />
        <TbChip Text="Open" Selected SelectedChanged="(bool _) => { }" />
    </TbStack>
    <TbList aria-label="Trails with your brand color">
        <TbListItem Selected OnClick="() => { }">Ridge loop</TbListItem>
        <TbListItem OnClick="() => { }">Creek path</TbListItem>
    </TbList>
</div>
<CodeBlock Label="app.css" Code="@BrandCss" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section" id="other-stylesheets">
    Other stylesheets
</TbText>
<TbText>
    The rule that lets your CSS win also lets any other stylesheet outside a layer win. A global rule such as
    <code>* { margin: 0; padding: 0; }</code>, which many resets and some component libraries ship, then strips the
    components' own spacing, and nothing warns about it: dialogs sit in the page's top corner, fields lose their inner
    spacing and radio buttons lose their dot. If you see that, look for an unlayered rule from another stylesheet.
</TbText>
<TbText>
    Put that stylesheet in a cascade layer of its own, ordered before Trailblazor's, so Trailblazor's styles win over
    it and yours still win over both. The order has to be stated before <code>TbStyles</code> loads Trailblazor's
    layers, and a stylesheet joins a layer when it is imported with <code>layer()</code>.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="App.razor" Code="@LayerOrder" />
<CodeBlock Label="app.css" Code="@LayerImport" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Your own palette</TbText>
<TbText>
    A palette is a block of color tokens for one value of <code>data-tb-palette</code>. Use
    <code>light-dark()</code> so each color has its light and dark value, and set the background, surface, text,
    border, primary and neutral tokens; the primary's states and the focus ring follow from the primary. The built-in
    palettes in <code>trailblazor.css</code> list every token a palette sets.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="A custom palette" Code="@CustomPalette" />
<TbText>
    Choose it by the name its rule matches, as you would a built-in palette: <code>new Palette("forest")</code> in
    <code>TrailblazorOptions.Theme</code> or <code>IThemeService.SetPaletteAsync</code>. It is applied before the page
    first paints and remembered like any other palette. For a picker of your own, <code>Palette.BuiltIn</code> lists the
    built-in palettes.
</TbText>
<CodeBlock Label="Program.cs" Code="@CustomPaletteOption" />

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Reduced motion</TbText>
<TbText>
    When the device asks for reduced motion, transitions finish at once and a busy button's spinner stops. An
    indeterminate progress bar or circle fills and gently pulses in place instead of moving.
</TbText>

<TbText TextStyle="TextStyle.Title" As="TextElement.H2" class="docs-section">Token reference</TbText>
<TbText>
    Every theme token, with the value the default palette and density give it, read from <code>trailblazor.css</code>.
    A color's sample follows the palette and mode chosen at the top of this site.
</TbText>
<TbTable aria-label="Theme tokens" Size="Size.Small">
    <thead>
        <tr>
            <th scope="col">Token</th>
            <th scope="col">Role</th>
            <th scope="col">Default</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        @foreach (var (name, value) in DesignTokens.Reference())
        {
            <tr>
                <th scope="row"><code>@name</code></th>
                <td>@DesignTokens.Role(name)</td>
                <td>
                    @if (DesignTokens.IsColor(name))
                    {
                        <span class="docs-swatch" style="@($"background: var({name})")"></span>
                    }
                    <code>@(value.Contains("from var(--tb-color-primary)") ? "From the primary" : value)</code>
                </td>
            </tr>
        }
    </tbody>
</TbTable>

@code {
    private const string DefaultBrand = "#0f766e";

    private string? _brand = DefaultBrand;

    private string Brand => _brand ?? DefaultBrand;

    private string BrandPrimary =>
        $"light-dark(oklch(from {Brand} min(l, 0.55) c h), oklch(from {Brand} max(l, 0.75) c h))";

    private string BrandCss => $$"""
        :root {
            --tb-color-primary: {{BrandPrimary}};
        }
        """;

    private const string Restyle = """
        /* Square buttons everywhere, and taller ones in the editor's toolbar. */
        :root {
            --tb-button-radius: 0;
        }

        .editor-toolbar {
            --tb-button-height: 2.75rem;
        }

        /* A chosen option in bold. */
        [data-part="option"][aria-selected="true"] {
            font-weight: 600;
        }
        """;

    private const string Theme = """
        @* App.razor *@
        <head>
            <TbStyles />
            <TbThemeHead />
        </head>

        // Program.cs
        builder.Services.AddTrailblazor(options =>
        {
            options.Theme.Mode = ThemeMode.System;
            options.Theme.Palette = Palette.Cobalt;
        });
        """;

    private const string StandaloneTheme = """
        <script src="_content/Trailblazor/theme-head.js" data-mode="system" data-palette="cobalt"
            data-density="comfortable" data-remember="true"></script>
        """;

    private static readonly (string Name, string Comfortable, string Compact, string Sizes)[] DensityTokens =
    [
        ("--tb-control-height", "40px", "32px",
            "Buttons, fields, selects, tabs, pagination and toggle groups; chips and calendar days are sized from it"),
        ("--tb-control-height-sm", "32px", "26px", "Small buttons, fields, selects, pagination and toggle groups"),
        ("--tb-control-height-lg", "48px", "40px", "Large buttons, fields, selects, pagination and toggle groups"),
        ("--tb-control-padding-block", "8px", "4px",
            "The space above and below the text in fields, tabs, list items and accordion headers; alerts use 1.5 "
                + "times this"),
        ("--tb-control-padding-inline", "12px", "8px", "The space either side of a button's or a field's text"),
        ("--tb-item-height", "32px", "28px", "Menu items, options in a select or list box, and tree rows"),
        ("--tb-row-height", "44px", "32px", "Table and data grid rows, list items and accordion headers"),
    ];

    private const string DensityOption = """
        builder.Services.AddTrailblazor(options => options.Theme.Density = Density.Compact);
        """;

    private const string DensityRegion = """
        <section data-tb-density="compact">
            ...
        </section>
        """;

    private const string Palette = """
        <section data-tb-palette="moss" data-tb-theme="dark">
            ...
        </section>
        """;

    private const string Overrides = """
        :root {
            --tb-font-family: "Inter", system-ui, sans-serif;
            --tb-radius-md: 2px;
        }
        """;

    private const string LayerOrder = """
        <head>
            @* Razor writes a literal @ as @@. *@
            <style>@@layer vendor, tb;</style>
            <TbStyles />
            <link rel="stylesheet" href="app.css" />
        </head>
        """;

    private const string LayerImport = """
        @import url("_content/OtherLibrary/other.css") layer(vendor);

        /* Your own rules stay outside any layer, so they win over both. */
        """;

    private const string Font = """
        :root {
            --tb-font-family: "Inter", system-ui, sans-serif;
        }
        """;

    private const string RegionPrimary = """
        <section style="--tb-color-primary: light-dark(#0f766e, #5eead4)">
            ...
        </section>
        """;

    private const string CustomPaletteOption = """
        builder.Services.AddTrailblazor(options => options.Theme.Palette = new Palette("forest"));
        """;

    private const string CustomPalette = """
        [data-tb-palette="forest"] {
            --tb-color-background: light-dark(white, #0f1a14);
            --tb-color-text: light-dark(#14231a, #e8f2ec);
            --tb-color-primary: light-dark(#1e6b45, #7fd1a5);
            --tb-color-on-primary: light-dark(white, #0f1a14);
            /* ...and the remaining surface, border and neutral tokens. */
        }
        """;
}
